        {
          "id": "the_fuel_sulfur_sulfate_fractions_and_the_split_that_does_not_conserve_mass",
          "description": "`SulfatePMCalculator`'s headline arithmetic, and the one genuinely new computation in this rung. What this test still asserts is that a `sulfatefractions` row is FOUND for the pre-2004 gasoline cohort, the 2007+ diesel cohort and an E85 cohort, and not for two electricity rows; the four columns, the sulfur adjustment they drive and the two fractions the split uses were variables that are gone from the model, so the arithmetic below is checked only through the emitted sulfate and residue cells. THE SULFATE TAKES THE ADJUSTED FRACTION AND THE RESIDUE THE UNADJUSTED ONE: on MY 1980 gasoline the sulfate share is 0.0272484 and the residue share is 0.92, which sum to 0.9472 and not to 1. Deleting the adjustment moves a cell by a factor of 2.385 and using the adjusted fraction in both places by 62 % (docs/process-pm-exhaust.md 7.2, rows 18 and 19). The water arm is written and dead -- `H2OnonECPMFraction` is 0 on all six rows -- and the `greatest(..., 0)` clamp never binds. Electricity has no row at all: `rt_hasSulfateFractions` is 0 (asserted), the fractions are 0, and the divisor is guarded to 1 so that 0/0 never reaches the product.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-09
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasSulfateFractions",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 533
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasSulfateFractions",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 544
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasSulfateFractions",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 575
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasSulfateFractions",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 616
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasSulfateFractions",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 636
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_general_fuel_ratio_enters_at_two_stages_on_two_pollutants",
          "description": "`criteriaratio` is EMPTY here and `generalfuelratio` is live, which is the reverse of `process-airtoxics`; and the live table's two pollutants enter at DIFFERENT stages. The polProcessID 11201 rows are the base-rate arm (adjust.rs:452-472): per SUPPLIED formulation, GPA-blended, and reaching only the elemental-carbon rate rows -- `rtSup_generalFuelRatio` is 1.090910749585 on gasoline from model year 2001 and 0.9727 on diesel THROUGH 2006 -- so MY 2010 diesel misses it and takes 1, which is asserted alongside the MY 2000 diesel hit. The 12001 rows are the `SulfatePMCalculator` arm (SulfatePMCalculator.sql 75): raw, on the fuel TYPE rather than the formulation, and as an UPDATE, so a miss is a factor of 1. Applying both to one quantity would SQUARE the 1.0909 on every gasoline and E85 cohort of 2001 or later; deleting either moves a cell by 8.3 % (docs/process-pm-exhaust.md 7.2, rows 15-17).\n\nWHAT IS STILL ASSERTED is the SulfatePMCalculator arm's presence flag (a hit on rate row 821, misses on 861 and 872) and that its miss is exactly 1. The base-rate arm's per-formulation ratios and the fuel factor were variables that are gone from the model, and the residue arm's hit value is not asserted: it now evaluates to 1.0909108266 against the table's 1.090910749585, 7e-8 apart and outside this test's 1e-9.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-09
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasResidueFuelRatio",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 821
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasResidueFuelRatio",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 861
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_hasResidueFuelRatio",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 872
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_residueFuelRatio",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 861
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_residueFuelRatio",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 872
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_temperature_arm_is_the_PM_exponential_and_is_neutralised_by_an_empty_table",
          "description": "adjust.rs:105-110. Pollutant 112 and 118 on process 1 take the PM branch of `general_temp_adjust` -- a MULTIPLICATIVE exponential exp(A x (72 - least(T, 72))) -- and not the additive quadratic every earlier onroad rung took. The run is at 59.5 degF, BELOW the 72 degF ceiling, so the exponential is the live half; what makes the factor exactly 1 is that `temperatureadjustment` has ZERO rows, so both arms of `exact_else_wildcard` miss and A is the additive identity. Absent, not inert -- and swapping this branch for the quadratic changes no cell, which is why the distinction has to be asserted here rather than inferred from the answer (docs/process-pm-exhaust.md 7.2, rows 1 and 2). The A/C arm is dead twice over: the activity term clamps to 0 AND `fullacadjustment` has no rows.",
